WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.120 --> 00:00:02.310 align:start position:0%
 
[Applause]  a show of force on the streets of sri  lanka's capital colombo  [Music]  public discontent has been simmering for  months over an economy close to  bankruptcy  [Music]  prime minister mahinda rajapaksa has  bowed down to pressure to resign  today's resignation is long overdue  but is unlikely to address the cause the  demands of the protesters so even  despite the curfew and a state of  emergency many are on the roads now  because they feel their calls their  demands need to be heard  demonstrators have rallied for weeks  outside the offices of rajapaksa and his  brother president gotta pay rajapaksa  demanding both step down  on monday morning a pro-government group  stormed a protest camp outside the prime  minister's office  attacking people with sticks and metal  bars and destroying the tents  cues for food fuel and medicine are  common for the 22 million sri lankans  the pandemic loss of tourism and rising  oil prices are all hitting hard  foreign reserves have dwindled to about  50 million dollars the monthly gas  import bill is 40 million dollars  the government has turned to the  international monetary fund for a  bailout  but that would take at least six months  to be finalized  a state of emergency and curfew have  been imposed and the military deployed  on the streets  but public anger remains and so do  demands for the president to follow his  brother and resign to  law about manly al jazeera  well let's speak to ahilan kadir gamar  about this he's a political economist  and joins us from jaffna in northern sri  lanka thank you so much for being with  us and for your patience so  after growing pressure the prime  minister has resigned do you think this  will be enough to appease protesters  no the demand of the protesters has been  that the  entire family who has governed this  country over the last three years should  resign and and crucial here is the  president the brother of the prime  minister who holds tremendous power uh  in fact soon after he came to power he  passed an amendment to the constitution  to keep even more power so until the  president goes i think these protests  are going to continue  but even if the president resigns i mean  even if the president resigns and and  the rajapaksa family is out of the  picture  that's not going to to address the main  issue here which is the economic crisis  sri lanka is facing today it's worse  since its independence  yeah this is going to be a very long  drawn out crisis but we need to  immediately start working towards  solutions  and  the president having lost all legitimacy  he just can't lead this country out of  this crisis in fact  the longer he stays in power we are  approaching a state of anarchy even  today when the prime minister resigned  his goons went and attacked  peaceful protesters and now  there is violence all over the country  and a  state of curfew has been  imposed so this kind of situation is  going to continue almost in a sort of  anarchic manner until the president  resigns and then of course they're going  to have to uh pick up the pieces and  really start working towards rebuilding  our economy and that might take five to  ten years even so we're going to see a  lot of political changes right over the  next  years in tandem with this economic  crisis right as laura mentioned in her  report before we came to you the  government has of course officially  requested emergency uh financial help  from the international monetary fund  which some people have said has come too  late now how might the political crisis  that is unfolding now affect the  negotiations with the imf  i think not just the imf sri lanka is  also trying to get support from india  and china and other countries just to  even get some  bridge financing to get over the next  six months but when there is so much uh  political instability uh it provides  very little confidence to even uh get  that kind of financing and on the other  hand uh you know with the strikes  tomorrow uh it's likely to be a national  day of protest and strikes that are  going to continue indefinitely right and  then the economy also gets affected  there so we have to get production going  our services back and while this  political crisis lasts it's just going  to make the economic hardship that much  more harsher tell us a bit more about  the economic hardship that sri lankans  are facing today because i mean public  conversations  on the crisis include of course a wide  range of issues from you know  agricultural policies and trade  regulation monetary foreign policy as  well and foreign credit sorry how does  that translate into everyday life for  the average sri lankan what what has a  crisis meant for people  so petrol and diesel are in short supply  the prices of those um fuel have also  doubled in the last couple months but in  in addition to that people have to wait  in hours to get  fuel cooking gas is in short supply in  fact a couple days ago  a lorry carrying cooking gas cylinders  was looted so um and then the price of  rice has doubled  uh bread is in short supply the price of  bread has doubled so both price hikes  and  shortages has really enraged uh the  people and and a lot of the anger that  we are seeing against  uh the prime minister and the and the  president is about the complete  mismanagement of the economy  thank you so much for talking to us  about this thank you for your time  ahilan  gama political economist and senior  lecturer at the university of jaffna in  sri lanka

